From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: wize-logic <wizelogic@proton.me>
Date: Mon, 19 Oct 2026 06:55:08 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] recomp: --x87-balanced keeps the caller's x87 positions in
 locals across a balanced callee

With --x87-locals and --callee-regs, a call to a function that touches the
x87 stack was a point where memory had to be the truth: the caller wrote
every stack position it had changed to g_fp_stack before the call and
loaded them all again after it, since the callee might push a result, pop
an argument, or read a slot below it.

Most callees that use the x87 stack do none of that: on every path they
pop what they pushed and no more, and they name only the slots they
pushed. x87_locals.x87_balanced follows a body's static depth through its C
text (the same rewrite the pass itself does) and says so; touched.balanced
marks such functions, with all they call, as a greatest fixpoint, so that a
function calling itself with x87 values live is balanced when its body is.

Around a plain direct call to a balanced callee the caller now writes only
g_fp_top (its depth, where the callee's own stack starts) and the
registers and x87 scalars the callee may touch; its stack positions stay
in their locals, with no store before the call and no load after it.
Where a body keeps the x87 state in memory (the registers-only fallback),
any callee whose registers are known is such a call.
